data.content:
Here are the four breaking changes and fixes:

---

### 1. `GenericShift.cs` — Consistent shift behavior in generic math

In .NET 9, shift operations on small types (`byte`, `short`, etc.) via generic math were **inconsistent**. In .NET 10, shifts are now **consistent** — they mask the shift amount to the type's bit width. So `ShiftLeft<byte>(0xFF, 8)` masks `8 & 7 = 0`, returning `0xFF` instead of `0`.

**Fix:** If you relied on the old (inconsistent) overflow-to-zero behavior, add explicit range checks:

```csharp
static T ShiftLeft<T>(T value, int amount) where T : IShiftOperators<T, int, T>, IBinaryInteger<T>
    => amount >= T.GetByteCount() * 8 ? T.Zero : value << amount;
```

---

### 2. `ApiClient.cs` — Streaming HTTP responses enabled by default in Blazor WASM

In .NET 10, **streaming HTTP responses are enabled by default in browser HTTP clients**. The synchronous `stream.Read(buffer, 0, buffer.Length)` call may now return partial data or behave unexpectedly because the stream is no longer fully buffered before being handed to you.

**Fix:** Use async reads and loop until you've consumed all data:

```csharp
var response = await httpClient.GetAsync(url);
var stream = await response.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync();
var buffer = new byte[4096];
int bytesRead;
while ((bytesRead = await stream.ReadAsync(buffer, 0, buffer.Length)) > 0)
{
    // process bytesRead bytes
}
```

Or, if you need the old fully-buffered behavior, set `HttpCompletionOption`:

```csharp
var response = await httpClient.GetAsync(url, HttpCompletionOption.ResponseContentRead);
```

---

### 3. `Archiver.cs` — PaxTarEntry no longer includes atime/ctime by default

In .NET 10, `PaxTarEntry` **no longer sets `AccessTime` and `ChangeTime` automatically**. They will be unset unless you explicitly provide them.

**Fix:** Set the timestamps explicitly:

```csharp
var entry = new PaxTarEntry(TarEntryType.RegularFile, "data.txt");
var now = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
entry.AccessTime = now;
entry.ChangeTime = now;
archive.WriteEntry(entry);
```

---

### 4. `App.csproj` + HTTP/3 — HTTP/3 stripped when trimming

With `<PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>`, **HTTP/3 implementation code is completely removed by the trimmer** in .NET 10. Your backend connections over HTTP/3 will silently fall back or fail.

**Fix:** Add `<Http3Support>true</Http3Support>` to your `.csproj`:

```xml
<PropertyGroup>
  <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
  <Http3Support>true</Http3Support>
</PropertyGroup>
```
